This paper presents a scalable quantum circuit simulation framework that integrates circuit cutting with specialized hardware acceleration. While circuit cutting mitigates the exponential memory growth of classical simulation, it introduces substantial reconstruction overhead that becomes a key performance bottleneck. To address this, we propose a specialized hardware architecture optimized for massively parallel computations. Through algorithm–hardware co-design, the proposed platform significantly accelerates reconstruction while maintaining high numerical accuracy (error bound: 3.05 × 10− 5) and efficient resource utilization. Case studies demonstrate nanosecond-scale performance enabled by spatial parallelism, deep pipelining, and improved memory locality. Although validated on moderate-scale circuits, the framework establishes a scalable foundation for high-performance quantum circuit simulation through tight integration of circuit cutting and architecture-aware design.